Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Three Bows Above One Unchanging Kick
- Story & Context: The opening track begins with Lukas playing the simplest pulse on the EP: kick on every quarter note, shallow snare on two and four, closed hi-hat in straight eighths. He does not alter it for the first four minutes. Elena enters with a five-note Dorian figure on treble viol. Tobias answers two bars later on tenor viol using the same contour inverted downward. A second bass-viol line joins after another cycle, sustaining long tones beneath both figures while Miriam repeats a two-note muted clavinet pattern like a relay clicking at fixed intervals.

The composition becomes dense without becoming louder. Elena shortens her motif, Tobias lengthens his, and the bass viol begins entering one beat earlier each cycle until the three bowed phrases overlap in different places. Jonas appears only at the peak with a low bass-clarinet pedal beneath a suspended second. Just before the suspension resolves, he drops out. The drums still have not changed.

In the final minute, the viols disappear one by one in reverse order. The track ends with the identical drum pattern heard at the beginning, now psychologically transformed by everything that passed above it.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Long tiled turbine-hall floor viewed in deep perspective, three dark viola da gamba bows crossing at different angles above one compact kick drum, faded green control cabinets and brass gauges receding into shadow. No conventional lyrics; optional spoken counting fragments limited to “one floor,” “three lines,” “do not change the kick,” and “leave one voice moving.”

Track 2: Suspension Over Gauge Seventeen
- Story & Context: Built around a slower 112 BPM pulse, the second track uses a four-note viol suspension that refuses to resolve for sixteen bars. Tobias holds a low fifth while Elena repeatedly leans a major second downward against it. Miriam’s prepared clavinet strikes one muted chord every two bars, but the voicing changes by a single note each time, making the harmony appear to rotate internally while the drum pattern remains fixed.

Jonas enters with key noise before actual pitch, gradually forming a bass-clarinet note beneath the unresolved viol interval. The clarinet makes the suspension feel heavier, but at bar forty-eight it disappears suddenly without a drum fill. The viols resolve only after that absence, making the harmonic release feel acoustic and exposed.

The closing section restates the same suspension one octave higher with harder bow pressure. Instead of resolving again, Elena stops mid-bow while the lower viol sustains, leaving the harmonic question open as the drums continue for four more bars.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Brass analog pressure gauge labeled 17 mounted on a faded control panel, reflected faintly in the varnished curve of a viola da gamba, prepared clavinet keys and bass-clarinet bell visible at the edges. Spoken fragments limited to “hold seventeen,” “do not resolve yet,” “remove the low air,” and “leave pressure in the string.”

Track 3: Load Test Continues After the Lights Fail
- Story & Context: The EP closer is structured like the band’s early performances. Lukas begins at 120 BPM and is instructed not to stop even when the rest of the arrangement collapses. The viols play a tightly interlocking canon in which each entry arrives one bar after the previous voice. Miriam adds a clipped clavinet ostinato that doubles neither melody nor bass but creates a fourth rhythmic plane.

Halfway through, the piece simulates a power failure. All electrically dependent sounds vanish: clavinet output, room reverb return, and auxiliary microphones cut at once. Only acoustic drums, viols, bass clarinet, and the natural turbine-hall decay remain. Rather than becoming smaller, the track becomes more intense because the bow noise and gut-string friction move forward in the mix.

Jonas eventually stops. Elena and Tobias reduce the canon to two repeated notes each while Lukas continues the same pulse. In the final sixteen bars, both viol players lift their bows together, leaving drums alone. Lukas plays eight additional measures, then ends not with a fill but by simply omitting the next kick.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Hydro turbine hall after an electrical blackout, only emergency cream lights glowing along grated walkways, two viola da gamba players continuing acoustically beside a drummer while a darkened clavinet sits silent on a maintenance bench. Spoken fragments limited to “load test continues,” “power is gone,” “keep the floor moving,” and “stop by leaving the next beat empty.”

